statement:
Let $k \ge 1$ and write $\omega_k = e^{2\pi i/k}$ for the primitive $k$-th root of unity. For each $a \in \mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}$,
\[
  \sum_{j \in \mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}} \omega_k^{\,j a}
  \;=\;
  \begin{cases}
    k & \text{if } a = 0, \\
    0 & \text{otherwise,}
  \end{cases}
\]
where each exponent $ja \in \mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}$ is taken via its canonical representative in $\{0, 1, \ldots, k-1\}$.
